em  in order to guarantee security this is  in contrast to other off-chain  protocols such as channels where the  operator or the intermediary  indeed needs to put huge amount of  collaterals in order to  guarantee security we will not go into  details how channels work here i would  just like to  point out this for for for people who  are familiar  uh with with channels and plasma  protocols  so what are the main features of plasma  protocols well  we expect that if the operator is honest  you will achieve huge  unchanged savings and that is indeed  true because the parties do not have to  submit their transactions  on check they submitted often this is  quite good  but we also expect that the malicious  operator cannot really steal coins  to achieve this parties need to solve  conflicts unchecked for example if some  data and availability attack happens  parties need to solve this answer which  means that users have to monitor both  the blockchain and often instead of the  system to see if they match and  everything checks out  there are quite a lot of variants of  plasma protocols that have been designed  this is a picture from 2018  and some of the projects here might be  abandoned already  but as you can see people have really  tried to produce specific plasma  protocols for specific purposes  but all in all we can categorize plasma  into two main categories  one plasma cache where users own tokens  similar to  cache and two fungible plasma  also known as plasma mvp or norcaster  users have accounts  similar to checking accounts and  as in real life implementing plasma cash  is easier than implementing fungible  plasma  well we have had cash for like thousands  of years but checking accounts have been  around for maybe  uh a few centuries at most  and we'll see later why this is also the  case here in these two variants of  plasma  in a bit more details in plasma cache we  assign  tokens to users for example token one  here belongs to user two  token two belong to user one and a user  can actually own multiple tokens  a fundamental plasma however is kind of  the other way around  users are assigned to the number of  tokens they own  so uh and this number can also be a real  number it doesn't have to be like  a natural number similar real world  cache  if if a user wants to withdraw k tokens  from the plasma cache system he needs to  withdraw them individually  so he needs to actually communicate a  lot  with a blockchain this is also the same  situation with cash if i go to a bank  and  if i want we want to withdraw a lot of  money in pay-perform  well i might just need to bring a  suitcase with me  however fungible plasma similar to real  checks is more prone to cheating  and that is what we'll explain in the  next slide  fungible plasma suffers from a  phenomenon referred to as mass exits  let's see this in an example assume li  is the latest state of the plasma system  but the operator turns malicious and  does not really explain  the new state li 1 to the users  now you might be wondering okay why is  this a problem  well what if a malicious user who is  colluding with the operator now  has a balance of 15. so the balance of  this user is increased to 50.  well this user can actually drain the  smart contract  he can if this user actually exits with  15 coins  there won't be enough coins available  for the rest of the users  to exit which means honest users must  exit before it is too late  this is called mass exits because  parties have to exit  immediately  recall that this attack this this mass  exit phenomenon is called by data and  availability  which is non-uniquely attributable  now why doesn't plasma cache suffer from  mass exits  well plasma cache offers something  called exit challenges  assume here that we have three tokens  assigned to alice bob and charlie  and the operator turns malicious and  says no no token 3 actually belongs to  me or another party that i know  no longer belongs to charlie  and he withholds this latest state of  the system  so we're again on the date and  availability attack  now if at some point this malicious user  tries to exit token3  charlie will get mad but charlie can do  something smart here he can say  well i own token 3 and li in  state i the id state of the ledger the  plasma ledger  so you're claiming that token 3 now  belongs to you  give me a proof that i transferred  token3  since the transaction must be signed and  forging  charlie's digital signature would be  impossible except with some negligible  probability  the adversary would fail here and can  indeed be punished  for for trying to steal charlie's coin  to sum up plasma cash translating plasma  cash transferring coins require digital  signature  which means that the malicious operator  cannot really steal coins  and hemisphere behavior can easily be  challenged over the blockchain  but fungible plasma is not like plasma  cache operator connected to increase  another user's balance  he can stop explaining the latest state  of the system to the users and users  cannot easily detect what went wrong and  they cannot easily challenge it  which brings us to the table that i  showed at the beginning of the talk  plasma cache suffers from large exit  sizes but does not suffer from  non-uniquely attributable mass exits  but fungible plasma although having  short exit sizes  suffers from non-nuclear attributable  mass exits  now you might be wondering are messages  a huge problem  well they indeed are because blockchains  have low throughput and cannot really  handle some intense in many exits  therefore exit timeouts must be large i  think in the plasma community they  usually refer to seven days which is  quite a lot compared to other systems  such as visa or normal banking systems  furthermore the attack that i just  described doesn't have any cost for the  operator  the operator must simply withhold data  and  the attack is not uniquely attributable  which means no one can be really  punished for it  now the main question is is this  separation inherent  in other words is there a plasma  protocol with short exercises and no  mass exits  well this brings us to our result we  answer this question  in our work and the answer is  no there is no perfect plasma protocol  i would refer you to our paper for the  full proof  and the detailed results but in short  we show that either we have a situation  like plasma cache  which requires large exits or we have a  situation like fungible plasma  which requires mass exits let me give  the main idea  behind this proof and the observation  that we had  assume now we have three parties where  alice has one coin bob has zero coins  and charlie has one  coin now alice  which means that actually the the total  balance of the  plasma contract is two now alice  sends one coin to bob which means that  the next state of the system would have  bob  with one coin furthermore the operator  doesn't publish this state to the honest  users which is charlie here  pop tries to honestly exit from the  system and he should be  able to do this everything went through  honestly  and he can exit one coin which means the  balance  of the system would be reduced by one  now alice can try to revert her state  towards stating an i and claim that she  never transferred any coins to bob  an attempt to exit naturally if alice  exits  charlie will not be able to exit so how  can we  prevent alice from exiting here well  either  it is clear from bob's exit that alice  cannot exit which means that we are in  some situation like plasma cache  where bob is exiting a token and a token  cannot be  exited twice or charlie must exit before  alice which would be which would mean  that you will end up in a situation  like plasma like fungible plasma  now let me try to extend this idea and  give more intuition behind our proof  until now we had only three parties but  what if instead of just three parties  we have two n plus one parties  we have n parties here  which we refer to as camp alice and n  party  which we refer to as camp charlie and  the alice  camp is chosen randomly by the operator  and corrupted  and the parties in camp charlie are  separated from the rest of the system so  they're under data and availability  attack  furthermore the total balance in the  system is 2n  instead of just two coins  now the parties in camp alice can send  all their coins  to bob bob can as before  try to exit all his new coins which  would mean  that the new state of the system would  only include  end coins  parties in camp alice can also claim now  that they never sent any coins to bob  now in order to guarantee security no  one  from camp alice should be allowed to  exit therefore the description of camp  alice  should actually be known to the smart  contract otherwise if even one person  from campbell's exits  there is one person from camp charlie  that is not able to exit anymore  hence the total communication with the  contract must be overall large why  because cap alice was chosen randomly  and a random subset cannot  be compressed more than some certain  amount  and and therefore in order to describe  this set to the smart contract  the overall communication must be large  which means that either bob's exit was  large or  people in camp charlie try to mass exit  note that for technical reasons we  assume communication locality which  means that users only communicate with  the operator  this is necessary and are not with each  other this is necessary in order to  exclude trivial  protocols where parties actually run  some consensus algorithm of chain  which is not the goal of plasma the goal  of plasma is to actually  scale the blockchain and running a  consensus algorithm of chain between  a huge number of users is not really  scalable  with this i would like to conclude this  talk  we initiate the study of lower bounds  for off-chain protocols  and we show that there is an inherent  separation between plasma cache  and fungible plasma which means that  both plasma variants have  their place and use cases for example if  the operator is semi-trusted  then fungible plasma would be much more  efficient than plasma cache  but in case the operator becomes  malicious  then plasma cache might be a better  choice  now regarding future works well we just  initiated the study of lower bounds for  off-chain protocol so there are  many things that can be worked upon and  improved  for example we only analyzed plasma  protocols but there are  huge class of other off-chain protocols  such as channels channel networks or  virtual channels  which can be analyzed furthermore  one can improve our results by by seeing  whether  for example this assumption regarding  communication locality can be changed or  removed  or try to change some of the modeling  choices that we made and see if  if the the same result can be achieved  with this i would like to  thank you all for your attention and if  you have any questions  feel free to contact me any of the  co-authors  or use the live panel to ask your  questions thank you very much
 

